<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chambal River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chambal is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vindhya-range">Vindhya Range</a> near Mhow in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> and flowing north-east through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>. Its lower course runs through deeply eroded ravines that long sheltered outlaws, and part of it is protected as the National Chambal Sanctuary for the gharial. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of Kota, Karauli and Dholpur lay along its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chambal-river.jpg" alt="Chambal River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kxnyshk · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chambal-river-gorge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
